1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,530
The data root is where a lab's big files live, on the lab's server, in folders with rules. This is what those folders are for.

2
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:29,107
Murmurent keeps memory in three tiers: what an agent sees in one session, what the oracle remembers, and the big files. Those live under one folder on the server, the data root, and protected or very large data stays there, never copied to a laptop.

3
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:54,695
Eight folders at the top and nothing else; inside each, one subfolder per project with the project's own name. So a repository called project x has immutable slash project x for its original data and append only slash project x for its outputs, and append only mirrors the repository's exp folder: code and data share a name.

4
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:06,695
Immutable is the data as it arrived: from the instrument, a collaborator or a public source. Once placed, nobody changes it, so every analysis traces back to it.

5
00:01:06,695 --> 00:01:24,003
Append only is where analyses write. New files and new versions are added, nothing is ever overwritten or deleted, so a result you reported last year is still there. Modifiable is the one place a project's data may change: working files, scratch.

6
00:01:24,003 --> 00:01:43,493
Mothballed is how the server makes room. You move files there that you no longer need on the server; they are sent to backup, then removed from mothballed. After that they live on the backup server only, not on the storage server. Release holds the lab's own copy of what it published: the papers and the software.

7
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:59,725
Cottage exists because home folders are small: large files that belong to a person, not a project, such as conda environments. One per login. Tools are programs the whole lab uses, db the databases several people share.

8
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:18,797
Inside Claude Code, hooks uphold the rules: a write into immutable, an overwrite under append only, a delete under the data root, all refused in these words. Guardrails for agents, not a security perimeter: a plain shell does not go through them.

9
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:32,459
One command checks the data root against the protocol; here on a demo with three mistakes planted. It reads names only. The PI decides; agents report and never tidy.

10
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:49,964
Name the folder when you ask an agent for work: original data to immutable, outputs to append only, working files to modifiable. Version, never overwrite. Big data stays on the server.
